% Provenance-bearing facts and judged templates (LE_extensions_proposal §3.1).
%
% "a damage is accidental" is open-textured: it is not derived by a rule but
% DECIDED, by someone, for a reason. The template says so ("; judged"), and
% the scenario that decides it records who decided, where it is written and
% why. Every scenario fact must say where it comes from, because the program
% declares "scenario facts require provenance".
%
% - scenario decided: the loss adjuster's judgment is recorded, so the claim is
%   payable outright; the explanation carries the three trailers.
% - scenario undecided: nobody has judged the damage yet, so the answer is
%   conditional and the open point is reported as a judgment needed.

the target language is: prolog.
scenario facts require provenance.

the templates are:
    *a claim* is payable.
    *a claim* is for *a damage*; undefined.
    *a damage* is accidental; judged.
    *a damage* is reported within the time limit; undefined.

the knowledge base judged damage includes:

a claim is payable
    if the claim is for a damage
    and the damage is reported within the time limit
    and the damage is accidental.

scenario decided is:
    claim one is for the burst pipe, as stated in the claim form at section 2.
    the burst pipe is reported within the time limit, as stated in the claim log at entry 7.
    the burst pipe is accidental,
        according to the loss adjuster, as stated in report LA-17 at page 3,
        because "corrosion was not visible on inspection and the pipe was within its service life".
    payable expects answers ["claim one is payable"].

scenario undecided is:
    claim one is for the burst pipe, as stated in the claim form at section 2.
    the burst pipe is reported within the time limit, as stated in the claim log at entry 7.
    payable expects answers ["claim one is payable"] and unknowns ["the burst pipe is accidental"].

query payable is:
    which claim is payable.

% A view (docs/le_summary.md §17.10): the claim file a claims handler works
% from — the facts with who states them, the judgment apart, what is still
% open, every claim with its outcome.
the view claim file is:
    the title is "Claim file".
    the case is a scenario, with the documents it is stated in.
    the facts about "the claim" are
        a claim is for a damage,
        a damage is reported within the time limit.
    the judgments are
        a damage is accidental.
    every fact shows who states it.
    the result is the answer to query payable.
    the result shows its citations.
    the result asks what is missing.
    the result can be flipped.
    the documents of the case are shown beside the facts.
    the cases are listed with their results.
